@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the two paths as a history of humanity
#
# One node, in its own file because it concerns neither the affect scales nor
# any tradition. It belongs to this directory because no transmission carries
# it: it begins in a datable work by a named author and is afterwards reported
# as the old meaning of the two paths.
#

pmc:TwoPathsOfKnowledge a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The branching of humanity into a right-hand and a left-hand path of knowledge"@en, "Die Verzweigung der Menschheit in einen rechten und einen linken Pfad des Wissens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that humanity divided at a datable point of its history into two opposed paths of knowledge, a right-hand and a left-hand one, and that the two magics called white and black were sown as seeds at that moment."@en ;
    skos:note "What is new here is not the pair of names, which stands in the orientalist literature from 1832 and is held as pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature. What is new is that the pair becomes a history: two parties, a moment of origin, and a lineage of adepts on either side. Everything the later occult milieu inherits — that there are two camps and that a person may join one of them — rests on this move and not on the naming."@en ;
    skos:note "Checked against the earlier work rather than assumed from it. The full text of Isis Unveiled (1877), both volumes, carries no occurrence of a right-hand or a left-hand path, and none of the tantric vocabulary the names come from. The usual account, which dates the introduction of the terms to 1877, is wrong by eleven years: the passage stands in the work of 1888. See prima-materia#58."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is presented as a quotation from a commentary of unstated antiquity, and it is the commentary rather than the author that later reporting cites. That is what pm:circulatesAs records here, and it is why this node is an origination and not an ordering: an ordering names what it arranges, and this one arranges nothing."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "Helena P. Blavatsky" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The ancient meaning of the two paths, credited to a commentary of unstated age rather than to the work of 1888 in which it first stands." ;
    dcterms:date "1888" ;
    dcterms:source "H. P. Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine I: Cosmogenesis (London 1888), in the discussion of the fourth race, at the sentence on the branching of humanity into the right- and left-hand paths of knowledge or Vidya" .
